# Specification Pattern

## Pattern Classification

```text
Origin:
Enterprise Design Pattern
```

Unlike Strategy, Factory, Observer, etc., **Specification is NOT one of the original GoF patterns**.

It became popular through:

```text
Domain-Driven Design (DDD)
```

and enterprise application design.

Today it is widely used in:

- Spring Data JPA Specifications
- Dynamic Search APIs
- Rule Engines
- Eligibility Checks
- Filters & Query Builders

---

## Category

```text
Behavioral Pattern
```

Why?

Because it focuses on:

```text
Evaluating business rules and decisions
```

rather than:

```text
Creating Objects
```

or

```text
Structuring Objects
```

---

# Why Specification Pattern Was Introduced?

Consider filtering applications in HawkEye.

Initially:

```java
if(status.equals("RED")
   && category.equals("API")
   && region.equals("US")) {

}
```

Business changes:

```text
Status = RED
AND Environment = PROD
```

Next month:

```text
(Status = RED OR Status = YELLOW)
AND Category = API
```

Next quarter:

```text
Status = RED
AND Region IN (US, INDIA)
AND Environment = PROD
```

The filtering logic keeps growing.

---

## Typical Symptoms (Most Important First)

### 1. Business Rules Mixed Into One Place ⭐

A service suddenly owns:

- Status rule
- Category rule
- Region rule
- Environment rule

Many independent business rules are mixed together.

---

### 2. Large Conditional Logic

```java
if(...)
if(...)
if(...)
```

or

```java
if(a && b && c)
```

becomes

```java
if((a || b) && c && d)
```

which becomes hard to read and maintain.

---

### 3. Business Rules Change Frequently

Today:

```text
Status = RED
```

Tomorrow:

```text
Status = RED AND PROD
```

Next week:

```text
Status = RED OR YELLOW
```

Requirements evolve continuously.

---

### 4. Rules Need Reuse

The same rule may be used in:

- Search API
- Dashboard
- Export Report
- Alert Engine

Copy-pasting conditions violates DRY.

---

### 5. Difficult Testing

Testing individual rules becomes harder when all conditions are buried inside large methods.

---

# Core Idea

Encapsulate each business rule into its own specification object.

Instead of:

```java
if(status.equals("RED"))
```

Create:

```java
RedStatusSpecification
```

Instead of:

```java
if(category.equals("API"))
```

Create:

```java
ApiCategorySpecification
```

Each rule becomes an independent object.

---

# Structure

## Specification Contract

```java
public interface Specification<T> {

    boolean isSatisfiedBy(T object);
}
```

---

## Individual Specifications

```java
class RedStatusSpecification
```

```java
class ApiCategorySpecification
```

```java
class ProdEnvironmentSpecification
```

Each class owns one business rule.

---

# Example

Application:

```java
App {
   status
   category
   region
}
```

Specification:

```java
class RedStatusSpecification
     implements Specification<App> {

   public boolean isSatisfiedBy(App app) {

       return "RED".equals(
                 app.getStatus());
   }
}
```

Usage:

```java
spec.isSatisfiedBy(app);
```

---

# Combining Specifications

One of the biggest strengths of this pattern.

Rules can be composed.

### AND

```java
spec1.and(spec2)
```

Meaning:

```text
Rule 1 AND Rule 2
```

---

### OR

```java
spec1.or(spec2)
```

Meaning:

```text
Rule 1 OR Rule 2
```

---

### NOT

```java
spec.not()
```

Meaning:

```text
NOT Rule
```

---

Example:

```java
statusSpec
    .and(categorySpec)
    .and(regionSpec)
```

This represents:

```text
Status = RED
AND Category = API
AND Region = US
```

---

# Main Problem It Solves

Before:

```java
if(a && b && c && d)
```

Business rules become harder to understand.

After:

```java
statusSpec
    .and(categorySpec)
    .and(regionSpec)
```

Rules become:

✅ Readable

✅ Reusable

✅ Testable

✅ Composable

---

# SOLID Principles Involved

## SRP (Single Responsibility Principle)

Before:

```java
AppService
```

owns all filtering logic.

After:

```java
StatusSpecification
```

owns status rule.

```java
CategorySpecification
```

owns category rule.

```java
RegionSpecification
```

owns region rule.

Each rule has one reason to change.

✅ Better SRP

---

## OCP (Open Closed Principle)

New rule required?

Before:

```java
Modify existing if conditions.
```

After:

```java
Create new specification.
```

Example:

```java
CriticalAppSpecification
```

Existing rules remain untouched.

✅ Better OCP

---

# Extension vs Modification

This is the heart of Specification Pattern.

Bad:

```java
if(status.equals("RED")
    && category.equals("API")
    && region.equals("US")
    && environment.equals("PROD"))
```

Every new rule modifies existing logic.

---

Good:

```java
statusSpec
   .and(categorySpec)
   .and(regionSpec)
   .and(prodSpec)
```

Existing rules stay unchanged.

A new specification is simply added.

---

# Does Specification Improve Performance?

## Common Misconception

```text
Specification makes filtering faster.
```

Not necessarily.

The primary purpose is:

```text
Maintainability
```

not:

```text
Performance
```

---

## Real Benefit

Specification improves:

✅ Readability

✅ Reusability

✅ Extensibility

✅ Testability

✅ Dynamic Rule Composition

---

## Where Performance Can Improve

Spring Data JPA Example:

```java
Specification<Employee>
```

can generate:

```sql
WHERE status='RED'
AND category='API'
```

Database performs filtering.

Indexes can be used.

Query optimization can be used.

However:

> The performance gain comes from the database query execution, not from the Specification Pattern itself.

