# HawkEye Real-World Example

Suppose filter metadata is stored in MongoDB.

```json
{
  "field": "status",
  "operator": "EQUALS",
  "value": "RED"
}
```

Tomorrow:

```json
{
  "field": "environment",
  "operator": "EQUALS",
  "value": "PROD"
}
```

Instead of:

```java
if(field.equals("status"))
```

```java
if(field.equals("environment"))
```

Create specifications dynamically.

Benefits:

✅ Dynamic filters

✅ Metadata-driven filtering

✅ Less code modification

✅ New rules added through extension

This is an excellent HawkEye use case.

